  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe core idea is to represent the optimization problem as a sparse amplitude modulation (SAM) problem in the Fourier domain. By decomposing the overlap integral into its frequency components using a Cartesian basis expansion, we can more effectively navigate the high-dimensional optimization space. This approach leverages the following key insights:\n\n- The objective function (overlap integral) can be minimized by strategically placing \"dipoles\" in the frequency domain\n- Using Landweber iterations as a metaphor for energy level transitions allows us to avoid local minima\n- The SAM representation with harmonic frequencies enhances exploration of the search space\n\nThe method differs from the original in three critical ways:\n1) Uses sparse amplitude modulation instead of continuous uniform mixing\n2) Employs mode separation in the Fourier domain to target specific spectral peaks\n3) Involves multi-scale optimization across octave bands\n\nThe algorithm will run in stages: band-pass filtering, amplitude modulation, and final convergence. This layered approach ensures both global and local optimization while maintaining the integral constraint.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nThe optimization problem is defined as follows:\n\nVariable: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x \u2208 [0,2] \u211d\nObjective: C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k))dx \u2192 minimize\nConstraint: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1\n\nDiscrete Representation:\n- Let n = 280 points\n- dx = 2/n\n- h_i = h(x_i) \u2208 \u211d (with 0\u2264h_i\u22641)\n- Sum constraints: (2/n) \u00b7 sum(h_i) = 1\n\nRepresent h in a basis:\n- Let h(x) = \u2211_{k=0}^{n-1} c_k \u03c6_k(x)\n- \u03c6_k(x) = sin(\u03c0 k x / 2) for base harmonics\n\nObjective function:\n- C\u2085 = (2/n) \u00b7 max_{lags} |\u27e8h, 1-h\u27e9|_L2\n\nThe optimization uses:\n- A sparse basis \u0394 for the modulation coefficients\n- Loss function L = max_{\u2113} (\u27e8h, 1-h\u27e9)_\u2113 - (1 - 1/n)\u2225h\u22252^2\n- Jacobian matrix J = [J_{i,j} = h\u1d62\u00b7(1-h\u2c7c)]\n\nThe solution is projected onto a sparse representation with smoothing terms and amplitude constraints.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nThe algorithm proceeds in phases:\n\n3.1 Initialization\n- If `initial_h_values` is provided, use it as base solution\n- Otherwise, generate random candidate with integral=1 and 0\u2264h\u22641\n- Normalization: center the candidate around 0 with fixed variance\n\n3.2 Phase 1: Fourier Modal Basis Optimization\n- For frequency \u03c9 in \u03a9 = {2\u03c0k/n for k=1 to n/2}:\n    a) Select subset of points based on power spectrum\n    b) Perform Landweber iterations:\n        - Forward: A \u21a6 A\u03c9 = A + \u03c9(A\u2297F)\n        - Backward: F \u21a6 F\u03c9 = this.exp(-\u03b1F) iteratively\n    c) Apply soft-thresholding to coefficients: 0 if |coef|<\u03b7\n    d) Renormalize to satisfy integral constraint\n\n3.3 Phase 2: Sparse Amplitude Modulation\n- Let m be number of modes (default 5)\n- Initialize coefficients \u0394_i,j randomly (-1 to 1)\n- Optimization:\n    min_{\u0394} \u2211_{i,j}| (\u0394_i\u00b7h_j)^2 - h_ij |\n    subject to \u2211_i|\u0394_i| \u2264 M\n    via alternating directions method of multipliers (ADMM)\n\n3.4 Phase 3: Mode Mixing and Balancing\n- For each harmonic \u03c9_k:\n    - Compute power P_k = |\u27e8h, \u03c6_k\u27e9|^2\n    - Balance modes: h_k*\u03c6_k = h_k*\u03c6_{k-1} * r (with r=0.8)\n- Final smoothing with Gaussian kernel (\u03c3=0.8)\n\nThe objective is evaluated via:\n    h_full = concatenate([best_h_opt, best_h_opt[::-1]])\n    corr = np.correlate(h_full, 1-h_full, mode='full')\n    C\u2085 = dx * |np.amin(corr)|\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n\nStandard Parameters:\n- n_modes = 5\n- \u03b1_Landweber = 0.05\n- \u03b2_SAM = 0.1\n- max_iter = 1000\n\nTo escape local minima, apply selective parameter variations:\n- Variation 1: Add random noise (\u03c3=0.05) if no improvement in 100 iterations\n- Variation 2: Swap two modes if plateau detected in Phase 2\n- Variation 3: Reduce frequency sampling rate if gradient too stable\n- Variation 4: Double the tolerance if plateau >500 iterations\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\nPhase 1: O(n log n) operations \u2248 2e3 operations\nPhase 2: O(m\u00b7n\u00b7max_iter) \u2248 5e4 operations\nPhase 3: O(m\u00b7n) \u2248 1e3 operations\n\nEstimate total operations: 2.3e7 (worst-case) in 100 seconds\n- Monitor computation time of each phase\n- Early termination if no improvement in final 10 iterations\n- Restart with new random basis if feasibility too low\n\nFallback procedure: If evaluation fails at any point, return mirrored initial parent solution.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n6.1 Feasibility Check\n- Verify 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- Verify sum(h_i)*dx = 1\n- If not, return dx * |np.amin(np.correlate(...))| but not the values\n\n6.2 Performance Metrics\n- Absolute improvement: best score since start\n- Relative improvement: (1 - C\u2085_prev/C\u2085)\n- Objective recheck with final solution\n\n6.3 Evaluation Boundary\n- The returned solution must satisfy:\n    np.all((0 <= h[i] <= 1 for i in range(n_points)))\n    abs(np.sum(h)*dx - 1) < 0.0001\n\n6.4 Return Format\n- h_values: np.array of optimized function (n_points)\n- c5_bound: final computed C\u2085 value\n- n_points: fixed at 280",
